1
00:00:00,000 --> 00:00:10,440
This is Doing and Feeling, the long version. Episode 9, The Map of Positions. In 1978, the philosopher Ned Block asked you to imagine the following.

2
00:00:10,800 --> 00:00:23,160
Take the population of China, more than a billion people. Give each person a two-way radio and one simple rule to follow so that together they implement the functional organization that describes your mind.

3
00:00:23,560 --> 00:00:27,840
Wire the whole network up to an artificial body and run it for an hour.

4
00:00:27,840 --> 00:00:35,500
If the right functional organization is all there is to having a mind, then during that hour the nation of China should feel what you feel.

5
00:00:35,760 --> 00:00:39,940
If you would be in pain, then something, somewhere, should be in pain.

6
00:00:40,600 --> 00:00:46,140
Block found that very hard to believe and argued that the burden of proof falls on the functionalist.

7
00:00:46,440 --> 00:00:48,280
Many people share the intuition.

8
00:00:48,780 --> 00:00:56,580
My intuition says no, but my intuition also says that a lump of wet meat should not be able to think, and here we are.

9
00:00:57,840 --> 00:01:03,480
science fiction story, they're made out of meat, in which aliens refuse to believe that humans

10
00:01:03,480 --> 00:01:09,660
think with meat. I'm not sure our intuitions about substrates are worth much. That's exactly the

11
00:01:09,660 --> 00:01:15,120
right worry to carry through this episode. Every position on this map is partly an answer to the

12
00:01:15,120 --> 00:01:21,180
question, which intuitions should we trust? Two episodes ago, Chalmers' six letters sorted the

13
00:01:21,180 --> 00:01:27,040
responses to the hard problem, and last episode gave the most radical one a full hearing. Today,

14
00:01:27,840 --> 00:01:33,500
of the territory. For each position, the core claim, the best argument, the strongest objection,

15
00:01:33,780 --> 00:01:38,140
and its verdict on the question this series builds toward. Could a machine be conscious?

16
00:01:38,500 --> 00:01:42,360
At the end, a short, clearly marked trip to the edges of the map.

17
00:01:43,520 --> 00:01:48,940
Start with functionalism, because it's the default view among philosophers who work on mind,

18
00:01:49,120 --> 00:01:54,760
and the implicit view of most people who build AI. Functionalism grew out of a problem with

19
00:01:54,760 --> 00:02:01,260
an earlier theory. In the 1950s, Ulland Place and JJC Smart proposed the identity theory.

20
00:02:01,480 --> 00:02:09,080
Mental states simply are brain states. Pain is a certain neural process, full stop. Then in 1967,

21
00:02:09,800 --> 00:02:14,760
Hilary Putnam pointed out that this was too narrow. An octopus can presumably feel pain,

22
00:02:14,960 --> 00:02:20,640
but its nervous system evolved independently and is built very differently. So if pain is

23
00:02:20,640 --> 00:02:26,860
one specific human brain state, the octopus cannot have it. Putnam's conclusion was multiple

24
00:02:26,860 --> 00:02:32,400
realizability. The same mental state can be realized by very different physical systems.

25
00:02:32,780 --> 00:02:37,700
So mental states should be defined by their function, by their causal role, rather than by

26
00:02:37,700 --> 00:02:44,020
their physical makeup. Pain is whatever state is caused by bodily damage, and in turn causes

27
00:02:44,020 --> 00:02:49,820
distress, avoidance, and the desire for it to stop. That's the octopus from episode 2,

28
00:02:50,220 --> 00:02:56,140
now doing philosophical work. Complex minds evolving more than once, on completely different

29
00:02:56,140 --> 00:03:01,060
hardware, is one of the strongest everyday reasons to find functionalism plausible.

30
00:03:01,660 --> 00:03:07,540
Computational functionalism is the stronger version. The relevant causal roles are computational.

31
00:03:08,420 --> 00:03:11,780
Implement the right computation, in neurons or silicon,

32
00:03:12,040 --> 00:03:14,720
and you get the mind, experience included.

33
00:03:15,000 --> 00:03:17,940
The hardest question for functionalism is one of grain.

34
00:03:18,260 --> 00:03:20,260
Which functional organization matters?

35
00:03:20,580 --> 00:03:24,260
At one extreme, you could define it purely by inputs and outputs.

36
00:03:25,020 --> 00:03:29,700
Block demolished that version in 1981 with a thought experiment now called Blockhead,

37
00:03:29,840 --> 00:03:32,740
a machine that passes any conversational test

38
00:03:32,740 --> 00:03:36,620
by looking up every possible reply in an astronomically large table

39
00:03:37,540 --> 00:03:43,960
advance. Behaviorally perfect, intuitively mindless. In fairness, that table would need

40
00:03:43,960 --> 00:03:49,700
vastly more entries than there are particles in the universe. And whatever large language models

41
00:03:49,700 --> 00:03:55,760
are, they are not look-up tables. They compress. That's the whole point of the training process.

42
00:03:56,300 --> 00:04:01,320
Agreed and Block conceded the machine was physically impossible. His point was that

43
00:04:01,320 --> 00:04:06,540
behavior alone cannot settle the question, so a functionalist must say which internal organization

44
00:04:08,000 --> 00:04:10,220
Too coarse, and you count blockhead.

45
00:04:10,540 --> 00:04:15,540
Too fine, down to individual neurons, and you exclude octopuses and machines.

46
00:04:16,000 --> 00:04:18,960
Whereinbetween turns out to matter enormously for AI.

47
00:04:19,420 --> 00:04:25,700
The best argument for functionalism about experience, not just thought, comes surprisingly from a dualist.

48
00:04:26,100 --> 00:04:31,820
Chalmers' fading qualia argument asks you to imagine your neurons being replaced one at a time

49
00:04:31,820 --> 00:04:36,580
by silicon chips that perfectly duplicate each neuron's input-output behavior.

50
00:04:37,540 --> 00:04:44,060
behavior never changes and you keep saying, I see a vivid red. If your experience gradually faded as

51
00:04:44,060 --> 00:04:50,220
the chips went in, then at some point you'd be having faded or absent experiences while sincerely

52
00:04:50,220 --> 00:04:56,240
reporting vivid ones, systematically wrong about your own mind with no internal sign of it. A

53
00:04:56,240 --> 00:05:01,500
companion argument, dancing qualia, imagines flipping between your neurons and their silicon

54
00:05:01,500 --> 00:05:07,220
twins with a switch. If experience depended on the substrate, your red could turn blue and back

55
00:05:07,540 --> 00:05:13,860
while you noticed nothing. Chalmers finds both scenarios implausible and concludes that experience

56
00:05:13,860 --> 00:05:18,880
depends only on fine-grained functional organization. He calls this the principle

57
00:05:18,880 --> 00:05:24,960
of organizational invariance. The objections are mostly intuition pumps, the china brain,

58
00:05:25,220 --> 00:05:29,460
the absent qualia worry that a functional duplicate might have no experience at all,

59
00:05:29,580 --> 00:05:34,320
and the inverted spectrum, the idea that someone could be functionally identical to you

60
00:05:34,320 --> 00:05:39,680
while seeing red where you see green. Functionalism's verdict on machines. Yes,

61
00:05:39,900 --> 00:05:45,300
a machine can be conscious if it has the right organization. The fight is over what right means.

62
00:05:46,280 --> 00:05:51,820
The second major physicalist family is representationalism, sometimes called

63
00:05:51,820 --> 00:05:57,840
intentionalism. The idea is that the character of an experience is fully determined by what it

64
00:05:57,840 --> 00:06:03,340
represents. When you see a red tomato, the redness is not a property of your experience.

65
00:06:04,320 --> 00:06:09,480
property your experience represents the tomato as having. The main motivation is called the

66
00:06:09,480 --> 00:06:15,560
transparency of experience, a point Gilbert Harmon made vivid. Look at a tree and try to attend to

67
00:06:15,560 --> 00:06:21,160
your experience of it rather than to the tree. Most people find they cannot. Every feature they

68
00:06:21,160 --> 00:06:27,240
notice, the green, the shape, the depth, is presented as a feature of the tree. Introspection

69
00:06:27,240 --> 00:06:32,820
seems to look straight through experience to the world. Michael Tai and Fred Dretzk built the best

70
00:06:34,320 --> 00:06:38,980
Classic objections involve cases where experience and representation seem to come apart.

71
00:06:39,240 --> 00:06:43,160
One is blurry vision, where the world is not represented as blurry.

72
00:06:43,640 --> 00:06:47,480
Another is Bloch's inverted Earth, a planet where colors are swapped.

73
00:06:47,680 --> 00:06:50,660
A traveler there, wearing color-inverting lenses,

74
00:06:51,240 --> 00:06:57,060
eventually comes to represent colors correctly while, intuitively, their experience stays the same.

75
00:06:57,460 --> 00:07:00,520
Here's why representationalism matters for AI.

76
00:07:01,200 --> 00:07:04,980
On one leading family of theories of what makes something a representation,

77
00:07:05,380 --> 00:07:10,180
called teleosmantic theories, a state represents what it was selected to track,

78
00:07:10,460 --> 00:07:12,660
typically by evolution or learning.

79
00:07:13,120 --> 00:07:15,720
Donald Davidson's Swampman makes the problem vivid.

80
00:07:16,180 --> 00:07:21,960
Lightning strikes a swamp and by sheer chance assembles a molecule-for-molecule duplicative view.

81
00:07:22,240 --> 00:07:26,740
On a strict teleosmantic view, Swampman states represent nothing,

82
00:07:26,740 --> 00:07:31,520
having no history of selection, so Swampman would have no experiences at all.

83
00:07:31,820 --> 00:07:36,320
Then the question for a language model is whether training counts as a history.

84
00:07:36,860 --> 00:07:41,900
Gradient descent is a selection process. It shaped every weight to track something.

85
00:07:42,320 --> 00:07:45,100
That's exactly what some philosophers have argued.

86
00:07:45,780 --> 00:07:51,420
Dimitri Quelyou, Malo, and Raphael Miliere, for instance, have argued that language models can

87
00:07:51,420 --> 00:07:56,340
have genuinely grounded representations, particularly once fine-tuning with human

88
00:07:56,340 --> 00:08:02,040
feedback, gives their outputs world-directed functions. So representationalism's verdict

89
00:08:02,040 --> 00:08:08,300
on machines is, possibly, depending on whether the system's representations are genuinely about

90
00:08:08,300 --> 00:08:13,460
the world, and on the further question of which representations count as experiential.

91
00:08:14,460 --> 00:08:20,620
Last episode's position, illusionism, belongs here on the map as the strongest form of type

92
00:08:21,920 --> 00:08:24,420
Its verdict on machines, in one sentence,

93
00:08:24,860 --> 00:08:27,840
a machine could be conscious in the only sense there is

94
00:08:27,840 --> 00:08:30,320
if it has the relevant kind of self-model,

95
00:08:30,500 --> 00:08:34,660
producing introspective representations of its own states for the right reasons,

96
00:08:34,900 --> 00:08:37,280
and not merely because it learned our words.

97
00:08:38,280 --> 00:08:42,680
Now a family that comes at the problem from biology rather than computation,

98
00:08:43,320 --> 00:08:43,800
an activism.

99
00:08:44,200 --> 00:08:51,000
In 1991, Francisco Varela, Evan Thompson, and Alina Roche published The Embodied Mind,

100
00:08:51,420 --> 00:08:55,360
arguing that cognition is not the manipulation of internal representations,

101
00:08:55,820 --> 00:09:00,240
but the activity of a living organism enacting a world through its body.

102
00:09:00,640 --> 00:09:06,180
It builds on autopoiesis, the self-producing organization we met in episode 4.

103
00:09:06,580 --> 00:09:10,560
Thompson's Mind and Life argues for a deep continuity between life and mind.

104
00:09:10,740 --> 00:09:17,880
In 2001, Kevin Oregon and Alva Noai proposed, in a Target article in Behavioral and Brain Sciences,

105
00:09:18,220 --> 00:09:20,360
that seeing is a kind of skilled activity.

106
00:09:21,420 --> 00:09:26,180
of the ways sensory input changes as you move. And Naya puts the slogan bluntly,

107
00:09:26,600 --> 00:09:30,420
consciousness is something we do, not something that happens inside us.

108
00:09:30,960 --> 00:09:36,220
Varela also proposed neurophenomenology, pairing rigorous, trained first-person reports,

109
00:09:36,400 --> 00:09:40,640
including from experienced meditators, with brain measurements so that experience and

110
00:09:40,640 --> 00:09:44,900
physiology constrain each other. That program comes back in the next episode,

111
00:09:45,440 --> 00:09:49,420
an activism's verdict on machines. Consciousness belongs to autonomous,

112
00:09:51,860 --> 00:09:54,640
Today's disembodied models are poor candidates.

113
00:09:55,400 --> 00:09:58,260
Artificial lifelike systems someday might not be.

114
00:09:59,300 --> 00:10:01,860
Next, biological naturalism.

115
00:10:02,240 --> 00:10:09,780
John Searle, who died in 2025, published The Chinese Room in 1980 as a target article with commentaries,

116
00:10:09,840 --> 00:10:14,640
and it became perhaps the most discussed thought experiment in the philosophy of AI.

117
00:10:15,160 --> 00:10:20,200
Searle imagined himself locked in a room with a rulebook for manipulating Chinese characters.

118
00:10:21,420 --> 00:10:27,180
in Chinese come in through a slot. By following the rules, he sends out answers indistinguishable

119
00:10:27,180 --> 00:10:33,040
from a native speaker's. But Searle understands no Chinese. Since a computer running a program

120
00:10:33,040 --> 00:10:38,680
does nothing more than he does, following rules for manipulating symbols, he concluded that running

121
00:10:38,680 --> 00:10:45,080
a program can never by itself produce understanding. In his slogan, syntax is not sufficient for

122
00:10:45,080 --> 00:10:51,540
semantics. The system's reply always seemed obviously right to me. Searle is one component.

123
00:10:51,760 --> 00:10:57,680
The room as a whole, the rulebook plus the person plus the scratch paper, is what understands

124
00:10:57,680 --> 00:11:03,800
Chinese. Asking whether Searle understands is like asking whether a single neuron understands

125
00:11:03,800 --> 00:11:10,200
English. That's the most popular reply. Searle's response was to imagine memorizing the entire

126
00:11:10,200 --> 00:11:16,000
rulebook and doing everything in his head. Now he is the whole system, and he still does not

127
00:11:16,000 --> 00:11:21,560
understand Chinese, he said. Defenders of the system's reply answer that he would then be

128
00:11:21,560 --> 00:11:27,040
implementing a second mind, when he has no introspective access to. Searle rejected that,

129
00:11:27,140 --> 00:11:33,360
and every other reply, including connecting the room to a robot body. His positive view was that

130
00:11:33,360 --> 00:11:39,420
consciousness is a real biological phenomenon, caused by the specific causal powers of brains,

131
00:11:40,200 --> 00:11:47,060
digestion is caused by stomachs. A simulation of digestion digests nothing. A computer simulation

132
00:11:47,060 --> 00:11:53,240
of a rainstorm leaves everyone dry. Functionalists have a reply to that last line, and it's worth

133
00:11:53,240 --> 00:11:59,820
carrying forward. A simulated storm leaves you dry, but a simulated calculator really does calculate.

134
00:12:00,340 --> 00:12:05,600
Some things are what they do, and some things are what they're made of. The question is which

135
00:12:05,600 --> 00:12:11,860
kind of thing a mind is, and that question will come back in episodes 12 and 13. The most developed

136
00:12:11,860 --> 00:12:17,320
contemporary version comes from the neuroscientist Anil Seth, who argues that consciousness may

137
00:12:17,320 --> 00:12:22,840
depend on properties living systems have and today's computers lack, such as metabolism and

138
00:12:22,840 --> 00:12:29,080
continuous self-production. Episode 13 gives that case its full hearing. The short verdict on

139
00:12:29,080 --> 00:12:34,900
machines, unlikely on current paths, more plausible only as AI becomes more brain-like

140
00:12:34,900 --> 00:12:43,520
or lifelike. Integrated information theory, or IIT, sits uneasily on the map. It's a scientific

141
00:12:43,520 --> 00:12:49,120
theory with radical philosophical commitments, so here's the philosophy, and in episode 11,

142
00:12:49,400 --> 00:12:55,580
the science. IIT, developed by Giulio Tononi and championed by Christoph Koch, starts from

143
00:12:55,580 --> 00:13:01,420
experience rather than from the brain. It identifies axioms, properties every experience has.

144
00:13:01,660 --> 00:13:08,300
It exists intrinsically for itself. It's specific, this experience rather than another. It's unified,

145
00:13:08,540 --> 00:13:13,920
not decomposable into independent parts. It's definite, with particular borders and contents.

146
00:13:14,120 --> 00:13:20,100
And it's structured, composed of distinctions and relations. Then it asks what a physical system

147
00:13:20,100 --> 00:13:25,580
must be like to account for each, and answers in terms of cause-effect power. How much the

148
00:13:25,580 --> 00:13:31,340
system's current state constrains its own past and future, as a whole, beyond what its parts do

149
00:13:31,340 --> 00:13:37,940
independently. That irreducibility is measured by a quantity called phi. The identity claim is bold,

150
00:13:38,180 --> 00:13:43,940
and experience simply is a maximally irreducible cause-effect structure. Three implications follow.

151
00:13:44,320 --> 00:13:48,940
First, consciousness is graded and widespread. Simple systems can have a little.

152
00:13:50,100 --> 00:13:55,900
an exclusion principle, only local maxima of integration are conscious. That means a collection

153
00:13:55,900 --> 00:14:01,640
of people, like a nation, is not conscious because its members are far more integrated than the whole.

154
00:14:01,880 --> 00:14:07,640
And third, the verdict that makes IIT famous in AI debates. What matters is the physical causal

155
00:14:07,640 --> 00:14:13,140
structure of the hardware, not the software. A conventional digital computer with its clocked,

156
00:14:13,320 --> 00:14:18,140
modular architecture has negligible integrated information whatever program it runs,

157
00:14:20,700 --> 00:14:24,460
That's the subtitle of Koch's book, The Feeling of Life Itself.

158
00:14:25,000 --> 00:14:27,780
Consciousness is widespread but cannot be computed.

159
00:14:28,160 --> 00:14:34,460
So on IIT, a perfect simulation of my brain on a GPU cluster is unconscious,

160
00:14:34,800 --> 00:14:40,120
but a large enough grid of simple logic gates might be more conscious than I am.

161
00:14:40,440 --> 00:14:45,760
Yes, the computer scientist Scott Aronson raised examples like that as a reductio,

162
00:14:45,980 --> 00:14:49,120
and Tone Oney accepted that a suitable grid could be conscious.

163
00:14:50,100 --> 00:14:55,340
IIT's verdict on machines, not conventional digital computers, whatever their behavior.

164
00:14:55,920 --> 00:15:00,880
Hardware built with the right physical feedback structure, such as some neuromorphic designs,

165
00:15:01,160 --> 00:15:02,580
could in principle be different.

166
00:15:03,600 --> 00:15:07,600
Then Type F, Russellian monism and panpsychism.

167
00:15:07,780 --> 00:15:13,100
In 1927, Bertrand Russell argued, and Arthur Eddington soon agreed,

168
00:15:13,320 --> 00:15:15,720
that physics tells us only about structure.

169
00:15:16,240 --> 00:15:20,560
How things relate and behave mathematically, never what they are intrinsically.

170
00:15:20,880 --> 00:15:26,360
Mass and charge are defined by what they do, which leaves open what it is that does the doing.

171
00:15:26,660 --> 00:15:33,900
The Russellian monist proposal is that the intrinsic nature is experiential or proto-experiential,

172
00:15:33,940 --> 00:15:38,280
and that's how consciousness fits into the physical world without breaking physics.

173
00:15:38,820 --> 00:15:44,420
Panpsychism follows if you think those intrinsic natures are themselves tiny experiences.

174
00:15:45,720 --> 00:15:51,840
In 2006, in a journal symposium that drew a long list of replies, Galen Strawson argued

175
00:15:51,840 --> 00:15:57,680
that real physicalism entails panpsychism, since experience cannot emerge from wholly

176
00:15:57,680 --> 00:15:59,040
non-experiential stuff.

177
00:15:59,440 --> 00:16:04,780
Philip Goff's Galileo's Error argues that modern science began by deliberately setting

178
00:16:04,780 --> 00:16:08,040
sensory qualities aside to make nature mathematical.

179
00:16:08,380 --> 00:16:11,920
And that consciousness is the bill for that decision coming due.

180
00:16:12,120 --> 00:16:14,840
The great problem is the combination problem.

181
00:16:15,720 --> 00:16:22,120
William James stated it in 1890. Take a sentence of 12 words, give one word to each of 12 men,

182
00:16:22,260 --> 00:16:28,800
and no one will be aware of the whole sentence. How do micro-experiences combine into one unified

183
00:16:28,800 --> 00:16:34,040
subject like you? Chalmers has distinguished several versions of the problem, the hardest

184
00:16:34,040 --> 00:16:40,420
being how subjects could sum into a new subject. Some panpsychists respond with cosmopsychism.

185
00:16:40,420 --> 00:16:46,340
The universe as a whole is the fundamental subject, and individual minds are aspects of it.

186
00:16:46,620 --> 00:16:50,260
The physicist Sean Carroll gave the sharpest scientific objection

187
00:16:50,260 --> 00:16:56,300
in a long conversation with Goff on his podcast Mindscape and later in a 2021 paper.

188
00:16:56,560 --> 00:17:00,720
The laws governing everyday matter, what Carroll calls the core theory,

189
00:17:01,080 --> 00:17:03,200
are tested to extraordinary precision.

190
00:17:03,460 --> 00:17:06,660
If consciousness made any difference to what particles do,

191
00:17:06,900 --> 00:17:09,440
those laws would be wrong somewhere we'd have noticed.

192
00:17:10,420 --> 00:17:14,700
no difference, it's hard to see how it could explain why we talk about consciousness.

193
00:17:15,500 --> 00:17:20,800
Goff's reply is that panpsychism does not change the equations at all. It's a claim about the

194
00:17:20,800 --> 00:17:26,160
intrinsic nature of whatever the equations describe. Carroll's rejoinder is that a nature

195
00:17:26,160 --> 00:17:31,620
that adds nothing to the dynamics does no explanatory work. Each side thinks the other

196
00:17:31,620 --> 00:17:38,460
has changed the subject. If electrons have tiny experiences, does my GPU get a bigger one?

197
00:17:40,420 --> 00:17:46,860
and that's often misunderstood. Most panpsychists think a rock is a mere aggregate of micro-experiences

198
00:17:46,860 --> 00:17:53,000
without being a subject itself. Whether a computer is more like a brain or more like a rock

199
00:17:53,000 --> 00:17:59,040
depends on the combination principle, which nobody has. So panpsychism's verdict on machines

200
00:17:59,040 --> 00:18:06,800
is genuinely open. Four more positions, more briefly. Idealism says experience is all there is,

201
00:18:06,800 --> 00:18:13,360
and matter is how mind appears from outside. Bernardo Kastrup's Analytic Idealism proposes

202
00:18:13,360 --> 00:18:20,180
a single universal consciousness that dissociates into individual minds by analogy with dissociative

203
00:18:20,180 --> 00:18:25,780
identity disorder, where one person can present several distinct identities. On that view,

204
00:18:25,980 --> 00:18:30,380
the question about a machine is whether it is a separate dissociated mind of its own,

205
00:18:30,520 --> 00:18:36,540
and nothing in the theory suggests that a computer would be. Dualism comes in two main forms.

206
00:18:37,320 --> 00:18:43,120
Interactionist dualism, Descartes' view, holds that a non-physical mind pushes the body around.

207
00:18:43,360 --> 00:18:50,000
It faces the problem Princess Elizabeth of Bohemia raised in letters to Descartes in 1643.

208
00:18:50,400 --> 00:18:56,460
How could something with no extension move matter? Modern physics adds the problem of causal closure.

209
00:18:56,920 --> 00:19:02,620
Naturalistic property dualism, Chalmers' view, adds experience as a fundamental feature

210
00:19:02,620 --> 00:19:06,520
tied to physical organization by laws we have not yet found.

211
00:19:06,940 --> 00:19:09,140
Remember the fading qualia argument.

212
00:19:09,740 --> 00:19:13,480
Chalmers thinks those laws link experience to functional organization,

213
00:19:13,860 --> 00:19:17,940
so his dualism, surprisingly, says yes to machine consciousness.

214
00:19:18,240 --> 00:19:22,820
The neighboring view, epiphenomenalism, makes experience causally idle,

215
00:19:23,100 --> 00:19:26,200
which raises a nasty puzzle, the metaproblem again.

216
00:19:26,540 --> 00:19:29,860
If experience does nothing, why do we keep talking about it?

217
00:19:30,560 --> 00:19:37,100
Mysterianism, from Colin McGinn in 1989, holds that there is a natural answer, but that human

218
00:19:37,100 --> 00:19:43,600
minds are cognitively close to it, the way a dog is close to calculus. A neighboring stance says

219
00:19:43,600 --> 00:19:49,320
phenomenal consciousness lies outside the reach of science altogether. The verdict on machines

220
00:19:49,320 --> 00:19:55,720
is that we may never know. And the quantum route. Roger Penrose argued in The Emperor's New Mind

221
00:19:55,720 --> 00:20:00,900
and shadows of the mind that human mathematical understanding is not algorithmic,

222
00:20:01,040 --> 00:20:03,400
using Gödel's incompleteness theorems.

223
00:20:03,660 --> 00:20:09,080
We can supposedly see the truth of statements that any given formal system cannot prove.

224
00:20:09,520 --> 00:20:13,880
Everyone I know in machine learning thinks the Gödel argument is wrong,

225
00:20:13,920 --> 00:20:16,320
but they usually cannot say why.

226
00:20:16,680 --> 00:20:17,740
Here's the why.

227
00:20:17,900 --> 00:20:22,200
The argument needs us to know that our own reasoning is consistent, and we do not.

228
00:20:22,420 --> 00:20:24,800
Most logicians reject it on those grounds,

229
00:20:25,720 --> 00:20:31,900
Rose paired it with a physical proposal, wave function collapse caused by gravity, which he calls objective reduction.

230
00:20:32,220 --> 00:20:41,580
With the anesthesiologist Stuart Hameroff, he proposed that orchestrated versions of this collapse happen in microtubules, the protein scaffolding inside neurons.

231
00:20:42,060 --> 00:20:43,200
Hence Orch-OR.

232
00:20:43,620 --> 00:20:45,900
The main objection is decoherence.

233
00:20:45,900 --> 00:21:00,140
The brain is warm and wet, and in 2000, the physicist Max Tegmark calculated that quantum coherence there should vanish in far less than a trillionth of a second, while neurons work on timescales of thousandths of a second.

234
00:21:00,820 --> 00:21:03,240
Hameroff and colleagues disputed the calculation.

235
00:21:03,560 --> 00:21:05,580
Then the physics met an experiment.

236
00:21:05,580 --> 00:21:16,080
In 2020, a team deep underground at Gran Sasso in Italy looked for the faint radiation that the simplest gravity-related collapse model predicts, and found none.

237
00:21:16,700 --> 00:21:25,520
A 2022 follow-up argued that under that simplest model, Orch-OR, becomes highly implausible, though more flexible versions survive.

238
00:21:25,520 --> 00:21:32,000
On the biological side, a 2024 study found that rats given a microtubule-stabilizing drug

239
00:21:32,000 --> 00:21:35,700
took about a minute longer to lose their writing reflex under anesthesia.

240
00:21:36,260 --> 00:21:40,380
Suggestive, but only eight treated rats, no quantum effects measured,

241
00:21:40,600 --> 00:21:42,640
and an anesthetic with many other targets.

242
00:21:43,200 --> 00:21:50,120
In 2025, the study's senior author published a review arguing that the experimental case is now strong.

243
00:21:50,420 --> 00:21:53,200
Most neuroscientists remain unconvinced.

244
00:21:53,200 --> 00:21:58,660
There are more cautious quantum proposals. Chalmers and the philosopher Kelvin McQueen

245
00:21:58,660 --> 00:22:04,340
have explored whether consciousness could cause collapse, combining IIT with a mathematical

246
00:22:04,340 --> 00:22:09,580
collapse model, and argued that versions not already ruled out could in principle be tested

247
00:22:09,580 --> 00:22:16,080
on quantum computers. And Tegmark himself proposed, in 2014, treating consciousness as a state of

248
00:22:16,080 --> 00:22:21,860
matter, which he nicknamed perceptronium, defined by how it processes information rather than by

249
00:22:23,920 --> 00:22:27,720
Orch-O-R-S verdict on machines is the clearest.

250
00:22:28,100 --> 00:22:30,340
Classical computers cannot be conscious,

251
00:22:30,600 --> 00:22:34,240
because consciousness requires a physical process they do not perform.

252
00:22:35,480 --> 00:22:37,580
Finally, a position about positions.

253
00:22:38,040 --> 00:22:40,440
Eric S. H. Witzgabel calls it crazyism.

254
00:22:40,720 --> 00:22:45,360
Every theory of consciousness, he argues, has at least one implication that sounds bizarre.

255
00:22:46,020 --> 00:22:48,240
Functionalism makes the China brain conscious,

256
00:22:48,800 --> 00:22:52,120
IIT certain logic gate grids, panpsychism electrons.

257
00:22:53,200 --> 00:22:57,880
Illusionism denies what seems undeniable, and dualism adds a new ingredient to physics.

258
00:22:58,280 --> 00:23:02,100
Something crazy must be true, we just do not know which crazy thing.

259
00:23:02,300 --> 00:23:06,560
His paper, If Materialism is True, the United States is Probably Conscious,

260
00:23:06,920 --> 00:23:09,720
makes the point with the China brain's real-world cousin.

261
00:23:09,960 --> 00:23:13,780
By criteria liberal enough to include rabbits and intelligent aliens,

262
00:23:14,220 --> 00:23:19,080
the United States, a richly integrated, self-monitoring, goal-pursuing system,

263
00:23:19,300 --> 00:23:20,220
would seem to qualify.

264
00:23:20,960 --> 00:23:24,080
IIT disagrees, thanks to its exclusion principle.

265
00:23:24,520 --> 00:23:28,900
Most readers find the conclusion unbelievable, which is precisely his point.

266
00:23:29,880 --> 00:23:33,560
Give me the scoreboard, which positions let machines in.

267
00:23:34,400 --> 00:23:38,600
Functionalism, yes, with the right organization and the fight is over grain.

268
00:23:40,140 --> 00:23:44,700
Representationalism, possibly if the machine's representations are genuinely about the world,

269
00:23:44,860 --> 00:23:46,640
which may depend on its training history.

270
00:23:46,640 --> 00:23:53,440
illusionism yes if it has the right self-model for the right reasons chalmers's naturalistic

271
00:23:53,440 --> 00:24:00,240
dualism yes by organizational invariance an activism and biological naturalism unlikely

272
00:24:00,240 --> 00:24:07,380
without something like life iit not on conventional digital hardware whatever it does panpsychism

273
00:24:07,380 --> 00:24:14,840
undetermined pending a combination principle castrop's idealism probably not orch o r not

274
00:24:16,800 --> 00:24:18,400
Mysterianism, we may never know.

275
00:24:18,660 --> 00:24:23,420
And the octopus, most positions on the map let it in, which is worth noticing.

276
00:24:23,840 --> 00:24:27,080
The disagreements cluster at machines, not at animals.

277
00:24:27,560 --> 00:24:28,460
Two more things.

278
00:24:28,700 --> 00:24:32,860
The positions that say yes do not all say yes to the same systems.

279
00:24:33,220 --> 00:24:37,600
A functionalist and an illusionist could disagree about a given language model.

280
00:24:37,840 --> 00:24:42,380
And the dividing line is not between scientific and unscientific views.

281
00:24:42,600 --> 00:24:45,520
It runs through the middle of the scientific community.

282
00:24:46,640 --> 00:24:52,560
Before we leave the map, a short detour clearly marked. The edges. These are ideas that are

283
